# On-Again, Off-Again: The Anatomy of a Cyclical Relationship

The relationship is over. You have said everything, blocked the number, told your friends it's finally done  - and there is a specific relief in that, clean as a snapped bone. Then, on night nine, the phone glows, and before you have decided anything, your thumb has decided for you, and by midnight the relationship is resurrected, and for three delirious days it is better than it has ever been. It feels like proof. The second breakup, when it comes, is harder than the first, and the third harder still, and somewhere in the middle of year two of this, you are no longer sure whether you are a person in a relationship or a person in a loop.

On-again/off-again relationships  - "cyclical relationships" in the research  - are startlingly common; studies of young adults find a large fraction report at least one breakup-and-renewal with the same partner, and a meaningful minority do it repeatedly. They are also, in aggregate, worse for the people inside them: research consistently links on-off cycling with lower satisfaction, higher uncertainty, more communication problems, and more anxiety than stable relationships or clean endings. But averages hide individuals, and the honest picture is more interesting than "on-off bad": the cycle itself is a diagnostic. *What kind* of on-off relationship you're in tells you whether it can become something stable  - or whether the loop is the relationship, and the only exit is through it.

## The Engine: Why the Reunion Always Feels So Right

The core of the cycle is not love, and calling it that is how the loop survives. The core is a **contrast effect**  - and understanding it defuses most of the cycle's rhetorical power.

When a struggling relationship ends, both people are in a state of deficit: grief, loneliness, withdrawal from a person their nervous system has spent years calibrating to. The misery is not evidence about the relationship; it is evidence about *attachment*, which would be equally miserable after leaving a good relationship or a bad one. Then comes the contact  - the text, the meeting  - and against the backdrop of that deficit, the ordinary warmth of the ex feels ecstatic. The relief is real, intense, and completely misattributed: it reads as *this person is amazing* when it is actually *withdrawal just ended*.

This is why reunions reliably produce a golden period  - the "honeymoon reunion," which cyclical couples know intimately: two weeks where the problems seem solved, where both partners are on best behavior, where the love feels bigger than the obstacles. The golden period is the cruelest feature of the cycle, because it is genuine *feeling* wrapping a false *prediction*. The feeling says: it's different this time. The base rates say: the problems that produced breakup #1 are still installed, and they will resurface on the same schedule as before  - usually by month three. And now the couple has an additional problem: each loop adds scar tissue (the breakups themselves become grievances), so the relationship is not returning to the same baseline but to a slightly worse one, each time.

There's a second engine worth naming: **reconciliation as ego-repair.** A breakup is not just a loss; it's a status injury  - someone chose to be without you. Reconciliation heals that injury instantly and feels, from inside, like destiny ("of course we came back to each other"). Much on-off behavior is less about the partner than about the unbearable sentence "they didn't want me," and the reunion is the appeal that overturns it. Watch for this in yourself, honestly: how much of the wanting-to-get-back is *wanting them*, and how much is *wanting to not have been left?*

## The Taxonomy: Three Kinds of On-Off

The single most useful thing about on-off research is the distinction between **transitioners** and **chronic cyclers.**

**Transitioners** break up essentially once, maybe twice, for a *solvable* reason  - bad timing, external pressure (the job across the country, the family opposition, the two people at different life stages), or a specific fixable behavior that genuinely got fixed (the drinking stopped; the communication course happened; the depression got treated). They reconcile, the reason is genuinely gone, and they join the population of ordinary stable couples; plenty of durable marriages contain one early breakup. For this group, on-off was a phase, not a nature.

**Chronic cyclers** loop because the loop *is* the relationship's structure: the underlying incompatibility is real, unsolved, and likely unsolvable, and the breakups/reunions are not addressing it. They can identify no reason the next round would differ from the last one. For this group, the cycle tends to continue until something external breaks it  - age, exhaustion, a third party's patience, occasionally a crisis. Left alone, it doesn't end; it *renews*.

**The third group is the most deceptive: the intermittent-reward couples**, who aren't quite cycling but aren't stable  - the threats of breakups, the breakups that last a weekend, the "we're done" that both parties know means "until Thursday." Here the breakup threat has become a *tool*: a conflict-ending instrument, a reset button, a way to extract attention or concessions. This is the most corrosive pattern of the three, because it converts the relationship's gravest instrument into casual vocabulary, and each devalued breakup makes a real ending harder to execute and harder to trust. Couples who cry "it's over" monthly are, functionally, training each other not to believe endings  - which is exactly why, when one of them finally means it, the other won't.

The diagnostic question that sorts you into the honest group: **between the last breakup and this reunion, what actually changed?** Not what *felt* different  - what changed in the world? A specific behavior, a specific circumstance, a specific commitment with evidence behind it? Transitioners can answer with nouns and dates. Chronic cyclers answer with adjectives and vibes  - "we've both grown," "the distance made us realize"  - and vibes are precisely what the golden period manufactures.

## Why Leaving a Loop Is Harder Than Leaving a Relationship

People outside the loop  - your friends, rolling their eyes at the third reunion  - underestimate the difficulty, because they're comparing it to an ordinary breakup. It isn't one. A cyclical exit is harder for at least three mechanical reasons:

**Every exit competes with a memory of a failed exit.** Your own track record is now a witness for the defense. The part of you that wants out knows that the part of you that wanted out last time didn't hold  - and that self-doubt lowers the cost of relapse. "I always go back" is not a fact about your future, but it *feels* like one, and feelings are what 2am decisions run on.

**The relationship's intermittent structure is literally addictive.** Unpredictable rewards  - reconciliation arriving, but only after the pain, and never on a schedule  - bind behavior more tightly than consistent rewards do. This is not a metaphor; it's among the oldest findings in behavioral psychology, and it's why slot machines and on-off exes use the same file format on the brain. The text that might come is the lever.

**The loop has eaten your social support.** After the third cycle, friends are reluctant to engage  - "didn't you two just break up?"  - and the embarrassment of another round pushes the cycler into isolation, and isolation pushes them back toward the one person who understands the whole story: the partner. The loop destroys the exits precisely because it keeps using them.

## Breaking the Loop

If the taxonomy has just shown you your reflection, the program has layers, from tactical to structural:

**Tactical: remove the lever.** The relapse almost always arrives through the contact channel  - the text at night, the "accidental" call, the drunk reply. The physics are simple: relapse requires contact, so make contact expensive. Blocked numbers (yes, really  - the block exists for exactly this), profiles muted, the mutual friends briefed ("don't tell me how they're doing; and please don't tell them anything about me"). People resist the block as "petty"; it is not petty; it is scaffolding. You are not punishing them; you are protecting a decision from a version of yourself that will exist at 1am on a Friday and have different, worse priorities.

**Interpersonal: if you reconcile, reconcile with terms.** If  - after honest assessment  - you believe you're a transitioner, then do what transitioners actually do: name the specific cause of the original breakup, and require *demonstrated* change, not promised change. "We broke up because of the drinking; I will come back when there's been a year of it not happening" is a reconciliation with a spine. "We broke up because we fought constantly; come back and we'll try to communicate better" is a loop with new paint. Also: couples counseling as a *condition of return*, not a thing to consider later. The research is unambiguous that on-off couples who reconcile without addressing the original cause re-break at high rates. Whatever brought you back must be bigger than missing each other, because missing each other is a given.

**Structural: pre-commit to the standard you'll use.** Cyclical couples make reunion decisions at the moment of maximum distortion  - mid-withdrawal, mid-flattery, midnight. The countermeasure is deciding your criteria *now*, in the calm, and writing them down: "I will only consider reconciliation if [specific behavioral evidence] has been true for [duration], decided in daylight, in a conversation, not over text." Then you hold yourself to consulting the document, not the feeling. It sounds bureaucratic. At night nine, when the phone glows, it is the only thing in the room that loves your future self.

**And if you're the one who keeps pulling them back:** the mirror version applies. Ask what you're actually summoning them back for  - the person, or the ending of your un-wantedness. If you cannot name what has changed since the last time, you are not asking for the relationship; you're asking for the pain to stop, and those are different orders, and only one of them can be filled by a person.

## The Rare Good News

To end honestly: on-off is not always pathology. A small share of these couples are transitioners whose timing was simply wrong at nineteen and right at twenty-six, whose one hard breakup was a bend in the road, and who go on to decades of boring stability. The difference between them and the loopers was never intensity  - the loopers have the market cornered on intensity. The difference is that when they reconciled, they could name what had changed, and it stayed changed. That is the entire test. If it's different this time, it will be because something is different this time  - out there, in the world, verifiable by daylight. If the only thing that's different is how it feels at midnight, then you already know, in the part of you that searched for this article, exactly what happens next.
$hx_body$
WHERE slug = 'on-again-off-again-the-anatomy-of-a-cyclical-relationship'
  AND (content IS NULL OR btrim(content) = '');

UPDATE public.posts SET
  content = $hx_body$
# Untangling: The Practical Ethics of Separating Two Lives

Nobody writes songs about the breakup's second act. The speech happens in an evening; the untangling takes months, and it is where most of the actual damage gets done. Two people who ended their relationship with dignity will spend the following six weeks discovering that they are still married to a lease, a car loan, a dog, a friend group, a streaming account, a retirement plan, and a garage full of jointly purchased power tools  - and every one of these is a conversation, and every conversation is a chance to re-fight the breakup.

The untangling deserves its own craft. What follows is the practical ethics of separating two lives, with the aim of arriving at the far side with your dignity, your finances, and  - where possible  - your friendships intact.

## The Master Rule: The Relationship Ended; the Partnership Didn't (Yet)

The single most useful reframe for this period: you are no longer partners in *life*, but you are still partners in *an enterprise*  - a small liquidation company whose only product is the clean end of a shared household. And enterprises are run very differently from relationships: they use schedules, written agreements, neutral venues, and dispassionate language. They do not use 2am feelings.

In practice this means three operating disciplines:

**Move all logistics to a business channel.** Text, not calls (calls carry tone; texts carry facts). Daylight hours only. If a conversation starts becoming emotional, either party may call a recess with a rescheduled time  - a right that must be honored, not treated as evasion. Some exes do this with a shared document: one running list of every open item, each with a proposed resolution, each item closed in writing. It sounds cold. It is cold. Coldness in logistics is not coldness of heart  - it's what preserves the warmth that the history deserves.

**Separate the emotional divorce from the material one.** The grief conversations (if you're having them) and the logistics conversations are different rooms. Do not bring grievance into the lease negotiation ("after what you did, you don't deserve the deposit") and do not bring logistics into the closure conversation. Mixing them is how a thousand-dollar dispute inherits the emotional weight of a three-year relationship.

**Timebox the process.** Open-ended untangling keeps two people entangled on purpose, sometimes for years  - and "we're still sorting out the storage unit" is a relationship wearing a moving-company uniform. Set a date: everything material resolved within sixty days, or it goes to a mediator. Then honor it.

## The Stuff: Dividing Without War

The possessions are the most obviously trivial part of the untangling and somehow the most fought over, so it's worth understanding why before setting rules. People fight over the coffee machine because the coffee machine is a *proxy*: it's the last arena where the relationship's grievances can still be adjudicated, a place where "winning" something feels like justice for everything else. Knowing this is armor. When you feel yourself going to the mat over the blender, you are not fighting about the blender, and the blender is not going to heal it.

Working rules:

**Yours/yours/ours.** Anything either of you brought into the relationship, or was given to you specifically (gifts, inheritance, family items), goes back to that person without negotiation. This is not a bargaining category. The only contested category is the genuinely joint: things bought together, for shared use, during the relationship.

**Joint items go to the person whose life needs them.** Not to whoever "deserves" them  - the deserving logic is how the proxy fights start  - but to *use*: the one taking the apartment gets the furniture; the one keeping the dog's routine gets the dog stuff. Need beats sentiment; sentiment loses either way.

**Buy, don't split, the sentimental standoffs.** For the few items both genuinely treasure, money solves it: one buys the other out at a fair price. It feels crude to price a memory. But the alternative  - a shared custody arrangement for one armchair  - is a tether, not a solution.

**The box rule.** Box up everything contested and small, exchange it via a friend or a neutral porch, and do the sorting separately. Prolonged joint sorting sessions are the single most common relapse-and-rewar zone in the entire untangling; a shared afternoon kneeling in a storage unit produces more regression than a dozen therapy sessions can undo.

## The Money: Being More Grown Than the Relationship Was

Money untangling has a hard edge because money is real, so three rules matter more than temperament:

**Honor the debts regardless of feelings.** The loan whose name is on it is legally whose it is; fair ≠ legal, and the untangling period is a bad time to discover the difference. Joint loans, co-signed leases, and shared accounts should be closed or refinanced into one name *as fast as possible*, because the ex-partner's default months from now is a form of contact you really don't want.

**Do not use money as a closing argument.** The pattern to avoid: re-litigating the relationship through the settlement  - the person who "paid more" asking for reparations, the person who left offering a buyout as apology. The relationship's emotional accounting is closed. Split the joint assets fairly, settle the joint liabilities by name, and leave the era's other ledgers unopened.

**Get the small formal things done early.** Beneficiaries (insurance, pension, will), emergency contacts, authorized users on cards, shared subscriptions, the family phone plan, the password manager. These are boring and they are exactly the details that ambush people years later  - the insurance payout going to an ex, the shared streaming account you're still paying for out of loyalty. Make a checklist, work it in one evening.

## The Hard Cases

**The lease and the home.** The person staying typically buys out the other's deposit and the mover documents the condition; the person leaving gives notice properly, in writing, to the landlord  - do not leave this to the stayer's word, and do not discover in three months that both your names are still on a lease with a person you no longer speak to. If neither can afford it alone, be adult enough to end the lease together, even if it inconveniences you.

**The pet.** The rule that works is not affection but architecture: the animal's *life*  - schedule, yard, costs, working hours  - belongs with whichever home it fits best, and visitation arrangements with an ex through a pet are a known trap: they are a subscription to the relationship for the length of the animal's life. If you share a beloved dog, share it honestly, with a schedule that both can hold for a decade. If what you actually want is the ex, the dog is not the way, and the dog deserves better than being a leash.

**The friendships.** The friend group is the untangling's quietest casualty, so it deserves explicit craft. Some rules that hold up: tell the group once, plainly, without prosecution ("we've separated; I know you love us both; please don't ask me to narrate it") and then release them from taking sides. Accept that some friends were really their friends and will go with them  - this is a real loss and must be grieved as one, not punished as a betrayal. And attend the weddings you're both invited to, when you can  - the poise of sitting near an ex at a mutual friend's celebration is one of adulthood's quiet graduations.

**The workplace.** If you work together, the operating rule is radical: *at work, you are colleagues, nothing else*. No processing in the hallways, no allies being recruited among teammates, no cold wars conducted through meeting invitations. The workplace version of the breakup must be so boring that a new hire cannot tell there was ever anything else.

## The Digital Layer

Modern untangling has a category no generation before us had, and it needs its own checklist: the shared cloud storage (photos!), the location-sharing, the shared passwords, the social-media connection. Location-sharing off, *the first night*  - it is the most violated boundary and the most understandable one, but knowing where they are at all times is not safety; it's a surveillance habit that re-tethers you at the exact moment you're trying to cut the line. Photos: split them once, kindly  - an agreed folder exchange, then done. And no breakup-posting, from either side, ever: the public score-keeping of the untangling is the move most guaranteed to convert a dignified ending into a permanent feud.

## What Clean Looks Like

The goal of all this is a specific endpoint: the state where the two of you can be in the same room  - a mutual friend's wedding, a shared pet's handoff, a workplace  - with no active hostility, no monitoring, no lawyer on retainer and no thread being kept warm. Not friendship; that's a separate essay and a later decision. Just *finished*: the enterprise closed, the books balanced, the story complete.

You get there by remembering, through every one of the hundred small negotiations, what the breakup speech already established: it's over, and the task now is not to win it but to *finish* it. The people who untangle well aren't the ones who feel the least. They're the ones who refuse to route their grief through the logistics  - who let the lease be a lease, and the blender be a blender, and the sadness go where it belongs instead of into the deposit. The relationship deserved better than it got at the end; that's why it ended. The untangling is the last thing the two of you will ever build together. Build it boring, build it clean.
$hx_body$
WHERE slug = 'untangling-the-practical-ethics-of-separating-two-lives'
  AND (content IS NULL OR btrim(content) = '');

UPDATE public.posts SET
  content = $hx_body$
# Boundaries Without Guilt: The Maintenance Guide Most People Never Got

There is a version of the boundaries conversation that has colonized the internet: identify your limits, say them clearly, and live free. What the posts never mention is the forty-eight hours that follow. You set the boundary  - the no to the family dinner, the unavailability to the friend who calls at midnight, the request to your mother to stop commenting on your body  - and then the reaction arrives, and with it the guilt, which is not a whisper but a weather system. You reread the message twelve times. You draft the apology. You wonder if you're becoming selfish, cold, one of those people. By Thursday you have softened the boundary into a suggestion, and by Saturday it no longer exists, and you have learned, once more, the lesson your whole life taught you: peace is purchased with compliance.

The missing piece of the boundaries genre is maintenance. Setting a boundary is one skill. *Holding it through the guilt* is a second skill, and almost nobody teaches it  - because the people who find boundaries easy don't understand the guilt, and the people who feel the guilt assume it's proof that the boundary was wrong. This essay is about the second skill. Its central claim is simple: **the guilt that follows a healthy boundary is not a verdict. It is a withdrawal symptom from a role you've been performing your whole life.**

## Why the Guilt Is So Loud (and So Misleading)

If you were raised in a family where your job was to be easy  - where compliance was the currency of love, where someone else's moods were the household weather you were responsible for managing  - then the act of declining a request does not register as a neutral choice. It registers as *abandonment*. As a failure of duty. Your nervous system learned early that a disappointed face near you was a danger state, and it still deploys the full alarm suite when you cause one: the anxiety, the rehearsing of the other person's disappointment, the urgent need to repair  - not because you were cruel, but because *someone is displeased and historically that was not allowed.*

This is why the guilt is so loud and so untrustworthy as a compass: it is not measuring whether you did something wrong. It is measuring how far you've strayed from the role. A person who spent thirty years being the family fixer will feel *more* guilt declining a manipulative request than a stranger would feel stealing a parking spot  - not because the boundary is worse, but because the boundary is a bigger betrayal of the old identity. **Loud guilt often marks the exact spot of the deepest training.** The guilt you feel about the hardest boundaries is frequently the guilt of the role dying, and the role's death deserves to be grieved, not obeyed.

The second mechanism is the *counter-reaction*. Boundary-averse people tend to be surrounded by people who have organized their expectations around your compliance. When you set a limit, those people don't experience it as self-care; they experience it as a service outage. Their pushback  - the sulking, the guilt trips, the "you've changed," the escalation  - is not feedback about your character. It is what an entitled system does when a subsidy is cut. Expect it, name it to yourself when it arrives ("this is the outage, not the verdict"), and let it pass through you without amending the boundary. The reaction is loudest precisely where the boundary was most needed.

## The Two Failures: Soft and Rigid

Before the maintenance mechanics, it's worth calibrating, because boundary problems come in two opposite forms and each misreads the other.

The **soft failure** is the familiar one: boundaries stated but not held, held but not survived, apologized for, riddled with exceptions until they are decorative. The soft failer loses the boundary but keeps the relationship  - and slowly loses the self instead.

The **rigid failure** is the shadow twin, often produced by overcorrection: walls described as boundaries, announced with moral grandiosity, held without repair. "That's a boundary" deployed to end any conversation that requires flexibility; limits never revisited as circumstances change; a new identity where every discomfort is reframed as toxic and every accommodation as self-betrayal. The rigid failer keeps the boundary and loses the relationship  - including the relationships that deserved the softness they stopped receiving.

The destination is the narrow road between: **firm on the limit, warm in the delivery, honest about the relationship continuing to matter.** A boundary is a fence with a gate, not a wall  - and the gate is what distinguishes self-respect from armor. Both failures share a root: treating the boundary as the end of the process. It isn't. It's the start of a negotiation you're allowed to have with yourself and with the people who love you.

## The Maintenance Program

**1. Pre-authorize the guilt.** Decide, before you set any significant boundary, that the guilt will arrive, that it is the toll, and that you will pay it *without changing the boundary*. Say it to yourself explicitly: "The next two days will feel terrible. Terrible is the cost, not the information." This sounds grim; it is the difference between the weather system and a decision. People who pre-authorize the guilt pay it once, like a fee. People who don't are ambushed by it and interpret the ambush as a moral emergency, then rush to undo the boundary that "caused" it.

**2. Separate guilt (I did something wrong) from discomfort (someone is unhappy because of a healthy choice).** This is a journaling-grade distinction, and it needs to be made in writing, because in your head the two are fused. Ask, concretely: did I cause harm  - or did I decline a role? Would a person I respect, watching this exchange, think I was cruel  - or just a person with limits? The answers are usually clarifying within minutes. Harm deserves repair. Discomfort deserves compassion, not capitulation.

**3. Hold the boundary with warmth, not apology.** The maintenance stance is not coldness. It sounds like: "I know this is hard to hear, and I'm not doing it to hurt you  - and it's still a no." The three banned appendages, because each one converts the boundary into a negotiation: the over-explanation (reasons offered become objections to be defeated  - say the boundary, offer at most one sentence of why), the apology-as-fee ("I'm so sorry, I feel terrible"  - which teaches the other person exactly how to invoice you next time), and the door-cracker ("unless that doesn't work for you"  - which is not a boundary; it's a request wearing one's clothing).

**4. Do not argue the boundary's legitimacy.** Once set, you are not obliged to defend it in a tribunal. The recurring argument ("but why can't you just this once") is not a request for understanding; it's a re-litigation strategy, because the arguer knows that if they can make you doubt the boundary's logic, they can make you doubt the boundary. The maintenance line for repeat offenders is kind and short: "I know it's inconvenient, and my answer is the same. How's the rest of your week going?" Redirect without retracting. Boundaries are kept by repetition, not by persuasion.

**5. Let the reaction be information.** The other person's response to your first held boundary is among the most honest data you will ever receive about the relationship. Respect, even disappointment-tempered respect ("I hate this, but okay  - tell me what works instead") is the profile of someone who was loving *you*. Rage, punishment, sulking-escalation, campaigns through family members  - that is the profile of someone who was loving the *service*, and their grief at its loss, while real, is not yours to fix. It is one of the most painful and clarifying audits available, and it only happens if you hold the line long enough to read the results.

**6. Keep the gate.** A held boundary is a standing preference, not a vow. As trust builds  - theirs in your firmness, yours in your own follow-through  - revisit the edges honestly: yes, the Sunday calls are still once a month, but the emergency exception you denied reflexively last week deserved flexibility, and you can offer it without the sky falling. Rigid forever-boundaries calcify into the other failure. The mature end-state is a fence you can lean on and a gate you actually open, on purpose, from both sides.

**7. Rebuild the identity the guilt is mourning.** Under the loudest boundary-guilt is usually a lost role: the easy one, the fixer, the endless well. That role paid you  - in belonging, in safety, in being needed  - and the guilt is its severance. Grieve it deliberately: write down what the role gave you, what it cost, and what you're replacing it with (a self that can be generous from choice rather than from fear; relationships that can survive your having edges). This is slow work  - months, not a weekend  - and it is the actual project. The individual boundaries are just the reps.

## The Special Cases, Honestly

**With parents**, the training is oldest and the role most fused with love. Two truths coexist: the boundary is necessary (adult children of boundary-crossing parents need limits as surely as any institution of the family will resist them), and love across a held boundary must be louder than in ordinary relationships  - the warmth has to carry more weight, because the limit disappoints people whose approval is the oldest currency you know. "No" to the Sunday visit, plus visible delight in their company the visits you do choose, works. "No" with the frost of a stranger does not, and hardens into the rigid failure.

**With partners**, boundaries are hardest because intimacy blurs the property line between selves. But the healthiest couples are precisely the ones where both people can hear "I love you, and no" without treating the second half as a contradiction of the first. A partnership where neither person can decline anything is not intimacy; it's a merger  - and mergers have no room for two people.

**And if you're the one whose boundary was just crossed by this article**  - if "you've changed" is the sentence that came to mind while reading, aimed at someone in your life who recently set a limit: worth sitting with the question of whether they changed, or whether they stopped performing a role you had priced into your budget. Both are possible. Only one of them, examined honestly, tells you whether you're grieving a person or a service.

## The Endgame

The goal of all this is a quiet, almost anticlimactic state that people who grew up compliant rarely believe exists: the ability to decline things  - even significant things  - and feel *fine* afterward. Not hardened, not armored, not "past caring": fine. Warmly, unremarkably fine, the way a person feels when their yes and their no both belong to them. The guilt never fully disappears; it just shrinks to its true size, from a verdict to a notification, and the phone keeps ringing while you live your life. That is what the maintenance was for. The boundary was never the point. The point was becoming the kind of person whose word to themselves is good  - and letting everyone who loves you learn, eventually and by demonstration, that the fence has a gate, and the person inside it is worth visiting.
$hx_body$
WHERE slug = 'boundaries-without-guilt-the-maintenance-guide'
  AND (content IS NULL OR btrim(content) = '');

UPDATE public.posts SET
